This file (icpc3.tsv) contains two components of ICPC-3, the International
Classification of Primary Care, Third Revision: S (symptoms, complaints and
abnormal findings) and D (diagnoses and diseases). The copyright of
ICPC-3, in electronic form and in hard copy, is owned by WONCA (the World
Organization of Family Doctors), published by the WONCA ICPC Foundation.

WONCA's own licensing page states the terms, and they are quoted here rather
than paraphrased:

  "Within all three forms of licensing the ICPC-3 is free to be used by any
   party under a Creative Commons License BY-ND. This includes the free use of
   ICPC-3 by commercial parties."
      -- www.icpc-3.info, "Licensing and contact", retrieved 2026-09-22

So using ICPC-3 in this package, and using this package commercially, needs no
agreement with anyone. The Official and Research Licenses described on that
same page are how national institutes, universities and companies support the
classification's maintenance and obtain translation rights; a Research License
is named as a requirement for a research institute or university in a country
without an Official License, which is a condition on institutions rather than
on this file. Nothing in this package depends on holding one.

  Publisher: WONCA ICPC Foundation, https://www.icpc-3.info/
  Code system URI: http://terminology.hl7.org/CodeSystem/ICPC-3
      (HL7 Terminology's NamingSystem-ICPC3. WONCA publishes no URI of its
       own; the secretariat has been asked to confirm.)
  Retrieved: 2026-09-22, from https://ct.icpc-3.info/download.php
      (the Coding Tool's own bulk download, GET, JSON, no key. The per-class
       browser API at browser.icpc-3.info/browse.php answers the same content
       about 1,100 requests at a time and is not used.)

WHAT THIS FILE IS
-----------------
1,218 rows, 331 in the S component and 887 in the D. One row per code: the
component and chapter it sits in, its class kind, and the classification's own
editorial content for it, the preferred term, the description, and the
inclusion and exclusion annotations. Columns: component, chapter, code, kind,
preferred, description, inclusions, exclusions, depth.

`translate_build/icpc3.py` fetches and builds it from one request to WONCA's
Coding Tool (`ct.icpc-3.info/download.php?language=english`, 10,886 rubrics),
and keeps that answer under `internal/indicators/icpc3/` as the evidence that
the table was not edited by hand.

Two things ICPC-3 publishes are deliberately NOT here. The other components
(interventions, results, functioning, administrative) are not shipped because
nothing in this package codes them. And every crosswalk rubric (SNOMED CT,
ICD-10, ICD-11, ICPC-1, ICPC-2, GBD, ICF) and the classification's index words
are not shipped because they are not ICPC-3's own words: measured over 3,139
index words, 1,273 (40.6%) are character-identical to a SNOMED CT description
listed on the same code once the reference and its semantic tag are removed,
and much of the rest carries ICD phrasing. Shipping them would put SNOMED CT
and ICD derivatives into this directory, which 1.5.0 emptied of exactly that.

NO DERIVATIVE WORK
------------------
CC BY-ND permits redistribution of the unadapted work and forbids publishing
adaptations. So: every term, code and annotation is byte-for-byte what WONCA
published. Nothing is translated, abbreviated, merged, re-worded or re-coded,
and no code is dropped from a component that is present.

Two changes, both mechanical and both applied everywhere. The rows are
serialised as TSV with LF endings and `;` between the values of a multi-valued
cell, which is the separator the source uses. And `<Reference>X</Reference>`,
which the API wraps around a cross-referenced code, is unwrapped to `X`: it is
markup rather than content, and left in it would put strings like "fear of
prostate cancer <Reference>GS94.00</Reference>" into a search index as a
surface nobody can type.

In particular there is no Chinese ICPC-3. A translation is an adaptation, and
WONCA licenses translations of the electronic version separately: "WONCA
handles the licenses for the translation of the electronic version of the
tabular list" (www.icpc-3.info, "Translations and Policy", retrieved
2026-09-22). `res/icpc3/symptoms_zh.tsv` is not a translation: it is this project's
own writing about which Chinese patient phrase points at which ICPC-3 code, it
holds no ICPC-3 term in any language, and it is Apache-2.0 with the rest of
the project. Display names shown to a user come from the `preferred` column of
THIS file, in English.

VERSION
-------
ICPC-3 publishes no release number inside the data retrieved above.
`mirobody.translate.icpc3.release()` therefore reports
`icpc-3+<12 hex>`, a digest over the parsed codes, preferred terms and
inclusions and index words of this file, so a coding names content that cannot disagree with
the stamp. Replace this file and the stamp changes with it.
